  "template": "myth-flip",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Today's mass-energy budget of the universe is about 5% ordinary matter, 27% dark matter and 68% dark energy.",
      "source": "Planck Collaboration, Planck 2018 results VI: Cosmological parameters, A&A 641, A6 (2020); NASA Science, 'Dark Energy, Dark Matter'",
      "checkedBy": null,
      "beats": [
        "l2",
        "l3",
        "l4",
        "l6",
        "l9",
        "l10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Stars in the outer parts of galaxies orbit faster than the visible mass can explain (flat rotation curves), a key line of evidence for dark matter.",
      "source": "Rubin & Ford 1970; Rubin, Ford & Thonnard 1980; OpenStax, Astronomy 2e, dark matter section (check section number)",
      "checkedBy": null,
      "beats": [
        "l4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Distant Type Ia supernovae are dimmer than expected, showing that the expansion of the universe is accelerating.",
      "source": "Riess et al. 1998; Perlmutter et al. 1999; The Nobel Prize in Physics 2011 (nobelprize.org)",
      "checkedBy": null,
      "beats": [
        "l6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Dark energy is the name for whatever drives the acceleration; neither dark energy nor dark matter has been identified.",
      "source": "OpenStax, Astronomy 2e, ch. 29 (check section)",
      "checkedBy": null,
      "beats": [
        "l6",
        "l8"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "simplifications": [
    "The shares are of the universe's total mass-energy today, not of its volume. They change over cosmic time.",
    "'Atoms' means ordinary (baryonic) matter: it also counts the gas between galaxies, which we mostly don't see. Neutrinos and light are a tiny extra slice, left out.",
    "Rotation curves are one line of evidence for dark matter; gravitational lensing and the cosmic microwave background are others.",
    "The numbers are rounded; 4.9 + 26.8 + 68.3 is the usual Planck split."
  ],
